When it's all done, give me a table: each tool, installed or skipped, where it lives, and anything still waiting on me.19:["$","pre",null,{"className":"rounded-xl bg-[#0a1020] p-5 font-mono text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[#e4e9f5] whitespace-pre-wrap break-words","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"$4c"}]}]
1a:["$","p","2",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Run it in Claude Code opened in your project, so it can see what you already have. It won't install all twenty on every project. A static website has no use for auth or a database layer, and an app that already runs on Supabase Auth shouldn't switch, so it tells you what it skipped and why."}]
1b:["$","p","3",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Some of these overlap on purpose. Gitleaks, Semgrep, Trivy, ZAP and the Claude security review all look for problems, but each one looks at a different layer, and the breakdown says which. OpenSpec and Task Master both plan the work before Claude codes it, and you probably only want one of them per project."}]
1c:["$","h2","4",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"1. Gitleaks: find the keys you already committed"}]
1d:["$","p","5",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Gitleaks scans your code and your whole git history for API keys, tokens and passwords. Deleting a key in a later commit doesn't remove it from the history, and anyone who can clone the repo can still read the old commit. Run it once on every repo you have, then add it as a pre-commit hook so the next key gets stopped before it's committed. If it finds a real key, rotate it at the provider, because removing it from the code leaves the old one working."}]
1e:["$","h2","6",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"2. Claude Code Security Review: a security pass on every pull request"}]
1f:["$","p","7",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Anthropic's GitHub Action reads each pull request for security bugs like injection, broken auth, exposed data and XSS, and comments on the lines it's worried about. It only looks at what changed, and it filters out low-impact noise before it posts. It needs an Anthropic API key saved as a repo secret. Anthropic says it isn't hardened against prompt injection, so on a public repo turn on approval for workflows from outside contributors. The same review runs on your machine when you type /security-review in Claude Code."}]
20:["$","h2","8",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"3. Chrome DevTools MCP: let Claude open the browser"}]
21:["$","p","9",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Google's MCP server gives Claude a real Chrome window. It can open your app, click through it, read the console, inspect network requests and record a performance trace. So instead of you copying an error out of the console and pasting it into the chat, Claude goes and looks. Claude can see everything in that browser, so don't log into anything personal in it. Google collects usage stats by default, and the --no-usage-statistics flag turns that off."}]
22:["$","h2","10",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"4. Better Auth: login that lives in your own code"}]
23:["$","p","11",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"An auth library for TypeScript that runs in your app and stores users in your own database. It's free and MIT licensed. Email and password, social logins, 2FA and organizations come built in or as plugins, and its CLI writes the database tables for you. If you already use Supabase Auth, Clerk or something else that works, leave it alone. Switching auth on a live app means migrating every user's account."}]
24:["$","h2","12",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"5. tweakcn: a shadcn theme that isn't the default"}]
25:["$","p","13",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Sites built on shadcn/ui all look the same because most of them ship the default theme. tweakcn is a visual editor for it. You start from a preset or change the colors, radius, fonts and shadows while it previews real components, then copy one command that installs the theme into your project. There's nothing to install until you've picked one."}]
26:["$","h2","14",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"6. Claude Code Action: tag @claude on GitHub"}]
27:["$","p","15",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"This puts Claude in your GitHub repo. Mention @claude in an issue or a pull request and it answers questions about the code, reviews the change, or writes the fix on a branch. It runs on GitHub's machines, so it works while your laptop is closed. Set it up by typing /install-github-app inside Claude Code. You need to be an admin on the repo."}]
28:["$","h2","16",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"7. Coolify: your own Vercel on a server you rent"}]
29:["$","p","17",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Coolify is an open source, self-hosted alternative to Vercel, Netlify and Heroku. You rent a VPS, run one install command, and get a dashboard that deploys from GitHub on every push, with SSL, databases and backups. You pay for the server, and there's no per-seat or per-build bill on top. The project recommends one server for Coolify itself and another for your apps."}]
2a:["$","p","18",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Until someone creates the admin account, the signup page is open, and whoever registers first controls the server. Create the account the moment the install finishes, or set the root user in the install variables so the page never opens."}]
2b:["$","h2","19",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"8. Trivy: known holes in your dependencies"}]
2c:["$","p","20",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Trivy, from Aqua Security, checks your dependencies, Docker images and infrastructure config against databases of known vulnerabilities, and looks for secrets while it's there. trivy fs . scans the project folder and tells you which version fixes each problem."}]
2d:["$","p","21",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"It's also the best lesson on this list. In March 2026 attackers used stolen credentials to publish a malicious Trivy release, v0.69.4, and moved almost every version tag of its GitHub Action to code that stole secrets from CI runs. It's tracked as CVE-2026-33634. That's why the prompt pins every GitHub Action to a commit SHA: someone with access can move a tag to new code, but a SHA always points at the same code."}]
2e:["$","h2","22",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"9. Umami: analytics without Google"}]
2f:["$","p","23",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Open source analytics you can host yourself. It doesn't use cookies or collect personal data, which makes it a lot simpler to deal with under privacy law than Google Analytics. Run it on your Coolify server next to a Postgres database, or use Umami Cloud. The first login is admin with the password umami, so change that before you do anything else."}]
30:["$","h2","24",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"10. ccusage: what your Claude Code usage costs"}]
31:["$","p","25",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Claude Code already keeps logs on your machine. ccusage reads them and shows how many tokens you used and what it would have cost on the API, by day, week, month or session. There's nothing to install: npx ccusage@latest. It also reads Codex, Gemini CLI, Copilot CLI and a dozen others."}]
32:["$","h2","26",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"11. Mem0: memory for the AI inside your app"}]
33:["$","p","27",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Mem0 gives the AI features in your app a memory. It saves facts from conversations, like a user's preferences or what they asked about last week, and pulls the relevant ones back into the next prompt, so your chatbot remembers people between sessions. Use the library from npm or pip, host the server yourself, or use their cloud. Skip it until your app has an AI feature that talks to users."}]
34:["$","h2","28",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"12. OpenSpec: agree on the spec before the code"}]
35:["$","p","29",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Spec-driven development for coding agents. Type /opsx:propose and a feature name, and it writes a proposal, the requirements, a design and a task list into openspec/changes/ for you to read before any code exists. /opsx:apply builds it, and /opsx:archive files the spec once it ships. The specs stay in the repo, so the next session can read why the code is the way it is."}]
36:["$","h2","30",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"13. Semgrep: bugs you can spot in the source"}]
37:["$","p","31",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Semgrep reads your source code for patterns that are known bugs, like SQL built from strings, eval on user input or a hardcoded secret. semgrep scan --config auto picks rules for the languages it finds. The CLI is free and open source, and there's a Claude Code plugin (/plugin marketplace add semgrep/mcp-marketplace) that runs it on what Claude writes."}]
38:["$","h2","32",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"14. ZAP: test the live app from the outside"}]
39:["$","p","33",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Semgrep reads your code. ZAP, which used to be OWASP ZAP, looks at your running app the way someone on the internet would, and reports things like missing security headers, cookies without the secure flags and error pages that leak details. The baseline scan only looks and doesn't attack, and it's one Docker command. Only point it at sites you own, because scanning someone else's site can get you in legal trouble."}]
3a:["$","h2","34",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"15. Drizzle: a database layer Claude can check"}]
3b:["$","p","35",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"A TypeScript ORM. You write your tables in TypeScript and query them with code that looks like SQL, with types all the way through. That gives Claude something to check its work against: if it queries a column that doesn't exist, the build fails before a user ever sees it. drizzle-kit writes the migrations. It works with Postgres, MySQL and SQLite, including Supabase and Neon, and Better Auth has an adapter for it."}]
3c:["$","h2","36",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"16. React Bits: animated components you copy in"}]
3d:["$","p","37",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"A free library of animated React components: text effects, backgrounds, cursors, galleries. Each one comes in JS or TypeScript and in CSS or Tailwind, and installs with the shadcn CLI, for example npx shadcn@latest add @react-bits/SplitText-TS-TW. It also works with the shadcn MCP server, so Claude can find and add components itself. Use one or two. A page where everything moves looks like a template."}]
3e:["$","h2","38",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"17. Claude Squad: several Claude Codes at once"}]
3f:["$","p","39",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Claude Squad runs several Claude Code sessions in one terminal, each in its own git worktree on its own branch, so they never edit the same files. Put one on the bug, one on the feature and one on the tests, check in on each, and merge what works. It runs Codex, Gemini and Aider too. It needs tmux and the GitHub CLI, and once installed you start it with cs in your project."}]
40:["$","h2","40",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"18. Marketing Skills: 53 skills for after you ship"}]
41:["$","p","41",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Corey Haines's set of 53 Claude Code skills for marketing: copywriting, conversion, SEO and AI search, pricing, launch plans, cold email, paywalls, analytics setup and more. Claude only reads a skill in full when the task matches it, so installing the whole set doesn't crowd every session. One command: npx skills add coreyhaines31/marketingskills."}]
42:["$","h2","42",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"19. OWASP Cheat Sheets: how to do the risky parts right"}]
43:["$","p","43",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"OWASP's free set of short pages on doing one security job properly: password storage, sessions, file uploads, JWTs, input validation and more than 130 others. There's nothing to install. The prompt adds one line to your CLAUDE.md telling Claude to read the matching cheat sheet before it writes code in those areas, so it follows the written standard instead of whatever it half remembers."}]
44:["$","h2","44",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"20. Task Master: a PRD turned into a task list"}]
45:["$","p","45",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Task Master turns a PRD into a list of tasks with dependencies, then tells Claude which one to do next. Write what you're building in .taskmaster/docs/prd.txt, ask it to parse the PRD, and keep asking for the next task. It runs as a CLI or an MCP server, and set to claude-code/sonnet it uses your Claude Code login, so there's no extra API key. If you went with OpenSpec, you can skip this one."}]
46:["$","h2","46",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"All 20 links"}]
